48
00:01:52,270 --> 00:01:54,190
There are a few key points here.

49
00:01:54,190 --> 00:01:56,250
Let's pull them out and talk about them one by one.

50
00:01:56,250 --> 00:01:57,790
The first one is polling.

51
00:01:57,790 --> 00:01:59,930
In other words, polling.

52
00:01:59,930 --> 00:02:01,590
By default, it does this every 30 seconds.

53
00:02:01,590 --> 00:02:04,050
A new card appears on the Linear board,

54
00:02:04,050 --> 00:02:05,970
and its status changes within those 30 seconds,

55
00:02:05,970 --> 00:02:07,410
Symphony will pick up on that.

56
00:02:07,410 --> 00:02:09,910
Each issue gets its own workspace.

57
00:02:09,910 --> 00:02:13,169
This means the workspaces won't interfere with each other.

58
00:02:13,169 --> 00:02:15,029
And it's preserved even after a run is finished.

59
00:02:15,029 --> 00:02:16,729
So the next time you run the same issue,

60
00:02:16,729 --> 00:02:17,829
and you can pick up right where you left off.

61
00:02:17,829 --> 00:02:20,389
If it fails, it will retry with an exponential backoff.

62
00:02:20,389 --> 00:02:22,749
So it won't just give up completely after a single failure.

63
00:02:22,749 --> 00:02:23,469
Symphony.

64
00:02:23,469 --> 00:02:25,100
It doesn't create tickets on its own.

65
00:02:25,100 --> 00:02:26,780
It only reads the status from Linear.

66
00:02:26,780 --> 00:02:29,220
Should it reply to the comment on Linear?

67
00:02:29,220 --> 00:02:32,800
Updating the status is something the agent does on its own using tools.

68
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:36,680
So, how is this different from Codex C2I or Co-Code that we're already using?

69
00:02:36,680 --> 00:02:37,990
So, what's the difference?

70
00:02:37,990 --> 00:02:39,490
There's just one difference.

71
00:02:39,490 --> 00:02:40,690
So, where's the entry point?

72
00:02:40,690 --> 00:02:42,830
Codex, CLI, or code.

73
00:02:42,830 --> 00:02:44,910
For those, the entry point is the terminal.

74
00:02:44,910 --> 00:02:46,120
